<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sasanian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sasanians, Sasanids, Sasanid Empire, Sasanian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sasanian Empire was the last pre-Islamic Persian empire, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> in the third century CE after overthrowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>. Centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, it was a great rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> as its state religion. It fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> in the mid-seventh century, but its administration and culture strongly influenced the Umayyad and Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire.jpg" alt="Sasanian Empire" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original file by Ro4444, edited by me · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sasanian_Empire_at_its_apex_under_Khosrow_II.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The loss of influence in Yemen to the <b>Sasanian Persians</b> in the 570s cut Aksum off from the eastern shore of the Red Sea and from the revenues of the trans-Arabian trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Sasanian Empire</b> Albanian cavalry served in Persian campaigns, and Ammianus Marcellinus records Albanians among the forces of Shapur II at the siege of Amida in 359.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under his son Muqan Qaghan (553–572) the state expanded greatly: the remaining Rouran were crushed, the Khitans defeated in the east, and Bumin's brother Istämi, ruling the western wing, destroyed the Hephthalites in alliance with <b>Sasanian Persia</b> and pushed towards the Caspian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After 474 the <b>Sasanians</b> paid 'an enormous tribute' until the 530s, and Peroz's coinage flooded Tokharistan; the Hephthalites developed no coinage of their own but minted Sasanian designs or countermarked Sasanian coins with their symbols.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its adoption of monotheism, and the strongly Jewish character of its court, made it one of the few states of late antiquity whose rulers favoured Judaism, and the religious conflicts of its final decades drew in the Aksumite Empire, the Byzantine Empire and later the <b>Sasanian Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 395 they launched their first large-scale attack on the Eastern Roman Empire, striking Thrace, overrunning Armenia, pillaging Cappadocia and entering Syria, while an invasion of the <b>Sasanian Empire</b> came close to Ctesiphon before a Persian counter-attack defeated it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
